sections.body:
Picture a folder called `website_final_v2_REALLY_final`. A change last Tuesday broke the menu, a teammate edited the same file, and nobody knows which copy is current.

A **version control system** records a project's history as snapshots, so you can:

- go back to any version and see exactly what changed;
- see who changed each line, when and why;
- work on ideas in parallel and combine them;
- share work without emailing zip files.

**Git** is **distributed**: every clone holds the full history, so you can commit, browse and branch offline. GitHub and GitLab host a shared copy and add code review.

